5792373/\n80:Source: Wan, Titman & Jaki, \"Subgroup Analysis of Treatment Effects for Misclassified Biomarkers with Time-to-Event Data\", JRSS Series C 2019. DOI: 10.1111/rssc.12364. arXiv: https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.00942. Publisher URL: https://academic.oup.com/jrsssc/article/68/5/1447/7058601\n90:Source: Küchenhoff, Mwalili & Lesaffre, \"A general method for dealing with misclassification in regression: the misclassification SIMEX\", Biometrics 2006. DOI: 10.1111/j.1541-0420.2005.00396.x. URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16542233/\n100:Source: Loh & Kim, \"Evaluating sensitivity to classification uncertainty in latent subgroup effect analyses\", BMC Medical Research Methodology 2022. DOI: 10.1186/s12874-022-01720-8. URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9508766/\n110:Source: Kull et al., \"Beyond temperature scaling: Obtaining well-calibrated multi-class probabilities with Dirichlet calibration\", NeurIPS 2019. arXiv: https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.12656. Proceedings URL: https://papers.nips.cc/paper/9397-beyond-temperature-scaling-obtaining-well-calibrated-multi-class-probabilities-with-dirichlet-calibration\n120:Source: Guo et al., \"On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks\", ICML 2017. arXiv: https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04599\n130:Source: van der Laan et al., \"Causal Isotonic Calibration for Heterogeneous Treatment Effects\", PMLR 2023. PMCID: PMC10416780. URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10416780/\n142:Mechanistic falsifiable hypothesis: In the exp002 DGP, replacing the current direct inverse of `P(Z_hat | Z)^T` with a prevalence-aware posterior mixing correction based on `P(Z | Z_hat)` will make oracle subgroup-specific correction improve GATE RMSE over uncorrected in high-error/high-CATE conditions, while the current direct inverse will not.\n150:Mechanistic falsifiable hypothesis: When subgroup membership is misclassified and treatment effects differ by true subgroup, a latent-class outcome model using the observed LLM label as a noisy proxy will recover subgroup GATEs with lower RMSE than hard-label uncorrected and global confusion correction because it estimates treatment interactions over posterior true-class weights.\n158:Mechanistic falsifiable hypothesis: A signed diagnostic that estimates the covariance between subgroup-specific correction residuals and subgroup CATE direction will predict oracle-global-worse conditions better than the current magnitude-only `mean(abs(centered_rate * centered_abs_cate))` score, because global correction error depends on the direction of contamination in the GATE vector.\n166:Mechanistic falsifiable hypothesis: Under sparse expert validation labels, hierarchical partial pooling over subgroup confusion-matrix logits will reduce GATE RMSE relative to independent per-subgroup confusion matrices by lowering variance, while still improving over global correction when true subgroup errors are heterogeneous.\n174:Mechanistic falsifiable hypothesis: Dirichlet-calibrated multiclass LLM modifier probabilities used inside a posterior-mixing correction will reduce subgroup GATE bias more than hard-label confusion correction when confusion is asymmetric across classes, because calibrated probabilities preserve uncertainty about near-boundary records that hard labels discard.\n182:Mechanistic falsifiable hypothesis: If the corrected H1 mechanism is real, MC-SIMEX extrapolation using the oracle categorical transition matrix will move GATE estimates toward the true GATE vector as added misclassification increases and extrapolates back, whereas the current exp002 inverse correction may move estimates in the wrong direction under heterogeneous row errors.\n216:No findings.md update was made. No experiment was launched.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
